First, I remember that the perimeter of a square is calculated by adding up all four sides. Since all sides of a square are equal, the formula for the perimeter (P) is P = 4 × side length (s). So if the perimeter is 44 cm, then I can find the side length by dividing the perimeter by 4. That would be s = P / 4. Plugging in the numbers, s = 44 cm / 4 = 11 cm. 

Now that I have the side length, the next step is to calculate the area of the square. The formula for the area (A) of a square is A = s², which means multiplying the side length by itself. So substituting the value we found, A = 11 cm × 11 cm = 121 cm². 

