Right, each digit multiplied by its place value (which is a power of 10). Now, the sum of the digits S would be:

S = a_n + a_{n-1} + ... + a_1 + a_0

The question is why if S is divisible by 9, then N is also divisible by 9.

So maybe I should look at N modulo 9. If I can show that N ≡ S mod 9, then since S is divisible by 9, N must be divisible by 9 as well.

But how do I relate the powers of 10 to modulo 9?

I recall that 10 ≡ 1 mod 9, because 10 divided by 9 leaves a remainder of 1. Therefore, 10^k ≡ 1^k ≡ 1 mod 9 for any integer k ≥ 0. That seems important.

So substituting back into the expression for N:

Each term a_i * 10^i ≡ a_i * 1^i ≡ a_i mod 9. So when you add all those terms up, the entire number N ≡ (a_n + a_{n-1} + ... + a_0) mod 9, which is exactly the sum of the digits S. Therefore, N ≡ S mod 9.

